hi there steve kaufman here today to  talk about language learning again  uh to exchange my views with uh those of  you out there who are interested in  learning languages  um today i want to talk about something  that i have mentioned before but i think  cannot be overstated  it's an extremely important  um part or should be an extremely  important part of our our language  learning strategy and that is the  importance of variety  and there are a number of reasons for  this first of all because to learn  anything our brains require a  combination of novelty  and repetition  so  that is always a part of my strategy  when i choose what kind of content i'm  going to listen to  um  but it's not just the novelty and  repetition that's good for our brains  it's the fact that when we have variety  it makes things more interesting and  keeping things interesting is also  extremely important in language learning  because that's what keeps you going it  leads to consistency it leads to staying  with it so you're achieving this sort of  freshness novelty because you're  changing the nature of your activity but  you're also and which therefore is  helping the brain learn but you're also  keeping it interesting and and you know  i often compare language learning to  sort of different fitness activities  so there's a concept in  in training that came up when i was a  youngster and was called fart lake  run it came from  the swedish and in swedish the word fart  means speed it doesn't mean  whatever it means in english and fartlek  means playing with speed so you'd go out  say jogging  but every so often you sprint and then  you go back to jogging so you're playing  with different speeds because  whatever you're doing whether it's the  brain or running if you kind of stay in  the same mode the whole time there is  sort of a declining efficiency  we talked about interleaving robert  bjork and his research on how  learning something leaving it a while  going back to something else coming back  to the first thing all of this helps you  in other words learning for getting  learning forgetting all of this helps  you to learn  so i noticed the reason i'm talking  about this is because i noticed that uh  in my arabic learning uh i had some i  had 10 online discussions with ibrahim  from egypt  and then i got a little bit tired of  struggling all the time  so i said i'll devote myself now to  uh  difficult content so i'm  fighting my way through these al jazeera  news articles that i import from the al  jazeera website into link and i go  through them and i'm looking up words  but there are fewer and fewer blue words  uh i'm now down to about 20 to 30  unknown words of course a lot of words  that i've met before i have no law i  have not yet learned but the whole  process gradually is one of more and  more words becoming familiar and then i  listen to the al jazeera podcast and of  course it's fun to listen to this  because it's lively and people are  talking and discussing and agreeing or  disagreeing  it's it's lively  it's a it's a  change from my many stories where it's  the same voice and there's a lot of  repetition and stuff so that's fun and  yet i don't really understand it  uh i'm now arranging to have some of  these transcribed by the way  so then i go back to my old mini stories  and lo and behold i understand them a  lot better it seems that they are now  spoken more slowly because i had that  the fast  audio from the podcast  where i i in fact was training my brain  to hear the words quite clearly even if  i don't know what the words mean  so then when i go back to my mini  stories not only do i hear the words  more clearly but i hear some of the  grammatical structures that this is a  third person plural or it's the it's a  female or it's a male or all these  things that i know about how the  language works but  i didn't always notice before when i was  listening to it but now that i have  exposed myself to faster material more  difficult material i go back to the  easier material and what do you know  it's easier than it was before so  what i wanted to emphasize and i've  mentioned it before but i think it's  it's it's worth stressing  vary  your activities of course focus on  things that are of interest to you  ideally but you may not be at a level as  this case with me in my arabic where i  can really make sense of the stuff that  is inherently interesting to me  so i do some of that  but then i go back to the easier stuff  and i had a few sessions  online talking with my tutor and so i  vary things i do the same when i go  through my review exercises on link i  will vary sometimes i'll have all five  the dictation the reverse flash card the  flash card the closed test the multiple  choice and sometimes i'll just do it  do two or three of them depending on my  mood so i'm varying it in many ways so i  just want to emphasize that in order to  keep yourself going keep the brain fresh  learn as effectively as possible  make sure you introduce as much variety  into your language learning as you can  so on that note i look forward to your  comments  [Music]  you
 

